src/backend/access/recno/README

RECNO Storage Access Manager
=============================

RECNO is a table access method for PostgreSQL that implements
heap-compatible xmin/xmax MVCC with in-place updates, integrated overflow
pages for large attributes, compression, and advanced space management.  It
provides an alternative to the traditional heap storage manager, using the
pluggable storage API introduced in PostgreSQL 12.

RECNO does not attempt to replace heap.  It is a specialized storage engine
designed for workloads that benefit from stable TIDs and reduced storage
bloat from in-place updates, integrated overflow pages, and built-in
compression.  It coexists with heap in the same database, and applications
choose the appropriate storage method per table.

Note: there is a Glossary at the end of this document.


Why RECNO exists
----------------

RECNO targets workloads where HEAP's out-of-place UPDATE (multi-version
page layout, HOT chains, index churn) is a poor fit.  RECNO carries the
same heap-shaped xmin/xmax MVCC fields and resolves commit visibility
through CLOG exactly like heap; what differs is the physical update model
(see "XID fields" and DESIGN section 1):

- **Update-heavy tables** that spend the majority of their VACUUM time
  reclaiming dead tuple versions produced by heap's out-of-place UPDATE.
  RECNO updates in place: the old version is pushed to a per-relation
  UNDO fork (zheap-shaped) instead of being left on the page, so TIDs
  stay stable, VACUUM has no HOT chains to prune, and index entries
  rarely need to be rewritten.  Snapshots older than the update
  reconstruct the prior version by walking the UNDO fork.

- **Bloat-sensitive tables** where the win is stable TIDs and in-place
  UPDATE rather than any change to the transaction model.

RECNO is an ordinary xmin/xmax access method: it stamps t_xmin/t_xmax,
consults CLOG for commit status, and freezes like any other AM.  It does
NOT escape XID wraparound -- anti-wraparound VACUUM and freezing apply to
RECNO exactly as they do to heap.

HEAP is not replaced.  RECNO coexists with HEAP in the same database,
and any individual table chooses one or the other at creation time.
The intent is that HEAP remains the default and RECNO is reached for
by users who have measured a specific pain point (bloat from update
churn) that RECNO's in-place-UPDATE design solves.


Design tradeoffs worth knowing
------------------------------

Everything RECNO gains from in-place UPDATE it pays for elsewhere.
The two tradeoffs that most often surprise operators are covered
in this section.

1. Rollback of in-place UPDATE / DELETE is asynchronous
--------------------------------------------------------

Because RECNO UPDATE overwrites the tuple on disk, the pre-update image
is not available on the page after the write commits to WAL.  The only
copy lives in the per-relation UNDO fork (see the RelUndo module:
`recno_relundo.c`, `src/backend/access/undo/relundo*.c`).  When a
transaction rolls back, two things happen:

  * **Immediately**, in the abort callback: `RecnoSLogXactCallback()`
    (`recno_operations.c`) marks the sLog entries for this xid as
    `ABORTED` via `SLogTupleMarkAborted()` (`src/backend/access/undo/slog.c`).
    But sLog is only an accelerator: CLOG is authoritative for the
    aborted xid, so MVCC visibility (`recno_mvcc.c`) treats the aborted
    xid's tuples as invisible to any snapshot regardless.  No reader ever
    sees the aborted write.

  * **Asynchronously**, from the logical-revert worker: the xid's
    per-relation UNDO chain is walked and each record is applied by
    `RelUndoApplyChain()` (`relundo_apply.c`, called from
    `recno_operations.c:7428`), which physically restores the
    before-image on the page.

Until the worker runs, an in-place-UPDATE-then-ROLLBACK leaves the
*physical* row in its post-update state.  Readers do not see it (CLOG
marks the xid aborted; sLog accelerates the check), but the on-disk slot
still holds the new image, so the space is not reclaimable by the FSM.
This is different from HEAP, where the old tuple version is still
physically present and reachable by snapshots with an xmin below the
aborted xid.

(The separate common-WAL UNDO path -- `UNDO_RMID_RECNO` ->
`recno_undo_apply()` in `recno_undo.c` -- exists for other UNDO clients;
RECNO row DML rollback does NOT emit those records and uses RelUndo above.)

For applications that commit/abort short transactions at
high rates, the backlog of physical undo work is small and the
`logical_revert_naptime` default keeps latency in the second range.
For long-lived aborted transactions or large bulk UPDATEs that
abort, the backlog can grow; operators should size the undo log and
the revert worker accordingly.  The visibility correctness is not
affected either way.

This is a deliberate departure from HEAP's multi-version-on-page
model.  It is the price RECNO pays for stable TIDs.

2. sLog concurrency and the partition count
-------------------------------------------

The sLog is the shared-memory structure RECNO consults on every
visibility check where `RECNO_TUPLE_UNCOMMITTED` is set.  Its tuple side
(keyed by `(relid, tid)`) is a partitioned, seqlock-guarded flat
open-addressing hash, not an LWLock-partitioned hash table.  Each
partition holds a single copy of its slice of the hash, guarded by a
per-partition sequence counter (`SLogFlatPartition.seq`) plus a
per-partition `writer_lock` LWLock.  Readers (`SLOG_SEQ_READ_BEGIN` /
`SLOG_SEQ_READ_END` in `slog_flathash.h`) acquire-load the sequence
counter, copy data into locals, then re-check the counter and retry if it
changed -- a wait-free retry loop, never blocking behind a writer.
Writers take the partition's `writer_lock` LW_EXCLUSIVE, bump the
sequence counter odd, mutate the single copy in place, then bump it even
again; this serializes writers within a partition but never blocks a
concurrent reader for longer than one retry.

The partition count is runtime-sized by the `slog_num_partitions` GUC
(`PGC_POSTMASTER`, default `0` = auto).  With the default, the tuple sLog's
`SLogComputeNumPartitions()` picks 4x the number of online CPUs, clamped
to `[SLOG_FLAT_MIN_PARTITIONS, SLOG_FLAT_MAX_PARTITIONS]` (16..256) and
rounded up to a power of two, so the count adapts to the host instead of
being pinned to a `NUM_BUFFER_PARTITIONS`-style constant.  Set
`slog_num_partitions` explicitly and restart if perf profiles show
writer-lock contention on a specific
partition count; the setting is fixed at postmaster startup.

The sLog also caps per-tuple concurrency at `SLOG_MAX_TUPLE_OPS` (128).
For a single TID this is the number of distinct ops (INSERT, UPDATE,
DELETE, LOCK_SHARE, LOCK_EXCL, ABORTED) from distinct transactions, plus
retained committed-UPDATE markers awaiting horizon advancement, that can
coexist before new ops are refused.  In practice this is never hit under
normal OLTP workloads, but malicious or degenerate uses of
`SELECT ... FOR SHARE` against the same TID from many concurrent backends
combined with a long-held snapshot can exhaust it.  On overflow, the AM
degrades gracefully to local-only tracking rather than raising an error or
blocking (see `flat_hash_apply_insert()`'s per-TID reclamation logic in
`slog.c`, which first tries to evict the oldest reclaimable retained
marker before giving up).


Key Features
------------

Heap-Compatible MVCC.  Uses the same xmin/xmax transaction-id visibility
model as heap, resolving commit status through CLOG.  A tuple carries its
inserter XID (t_xmin) and deleter/updater XID (t_xmax); older snapshots
reconstruct the prior version from the per-relation UNDO fork.  RECNO
freezes and is subject to XID wraparound exactly like a normal xmin/xmax
access method.  Supports all PostgreSQL isolation levels.

In-Place Updates.  Updates tuples in place when possible, avoiding tuple
versioning.  Significantly reduces storage bloat compared to heap (40-60%
for update-heavy workloads).  Falls back to new-page allocation when the
updated tuple does not fit.  Stable TIDs eliminate the need for HOT chains
and reduce index update frequency.

Integrated Overflow Pages.  Replaces TOAST with a Derby-inspired overflow
page design that keeps overflow data within the same relation.  Uses spatial
locality optimization (multiple overflow records per page) to reduce I/O
during chain traversal.

Built-in Compression.  Attribute-level compression with multiple algorithm
support: LZ4, ZSTD, delta compression for numerics, and dictionary
compression for text data.  Automatic algorithm selection based on data
type and pattern.  The codecs are fully implemented in recno_compress.c
(LZ4 and ZSTD are wired to the system libraries when the server is built
with USE_LZ4 / USE_ZSTD; if a library is unavailable the corresponding
codec falls back to storing the attribute uncompressed).

Advanced Space Management.  Five-category free space classification
(FULL/TIGHT/MEDIUM/LOOSE/EMPTY).  Proactive page defragmentation during
normal operations.  Integrates with PostgreSQL's standard FSM.

WAL-based UNDO/REDO.  Complete crash recovery with before/after images for
updates.  All page-modifying operations are WAL-logged.


Architecture
============


Page Format
-----------

RECNO pages use a custom format optimized for in-place updates:

    +------------------+
    | Page Header      |  32 bytes (standard 24 + RECNO metadata 8)
    +------------------+
    | Item Pointers    |  Variable, point to tuple locations
    +------------------+
    | ... Free Space ...|  Available for new tuples
    +------------------+
    | Tuple Data       |  Variable, actual tuple storage
    +------------------+

Page header fields include the standard PostgreSQL page header (24 bytes),
the page-level commit-ts bookkeeping word (8 bytes; a cosmetic hint, not
load-bearing for visibility -- read visibility comes from CLOG), free space
tracking (2 bytes), defragmentation counter (2 bytes), and page flags
(4 bytes) for compression, overflow, and defragmentation state.


Tuple Format
------------

RECNO tuples carry heap-shaped xmin/xmax MVCC fields plus a version-chain
pointer into the per-relation UNDO fork.  The fixed header
(`RecnoTupleHeader`, `recno.h`):

    Commit-ts word       8 bytes   low 32 bits = t_xmax (deleter/updater
                                  XID); high 32 bits reserved.  Accessed via
                                  RecnoTupleGetXmax/SetXmax.  Also used for a
                                  page-level commit-ts bookkeeping hint.
    Version-chain ptr    8 bytes   RelUndoRecPtr into the UNDO fork; head of
                                  this tuple's persistent version chain
                                  (InvalidRelUndoRecPtr = never updated)
    Writer lock          4 bytes   Per-tuple atomic CAS writer lock (0=free;
                                  else MyProcNumber+1 of the current writer)
    Inserter XID         4 bytes   t_xmin (heap xmin semantics)
    Current TID          6 bytes   ItemPointerData, points to latest version
    Attribute count      2 bytes   Number of attributes
    Flags                2 bytes   Deleted, updated, compressed, overflow,
                                  uncommitted, etc.
    Info mask            1 byte    Null attributes, variable length, external
                                  storage, compression
    Null bitmap          variable  Null bitmap, followed by an optional
                                  RecnoInlineDiff when HAS_INLINE_DIFF is set

Visibility is heap-shaped: t_xmin (inserter) and t_xmax (deleter/updater,
in the low 32 bits of the commit-ts word) resolve against CLOG and the
reader's snapshot exactly like HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC.  There is no
command-ID field in the header: the command ID is obtained from the sLog
entry (`SLogTupleOp.cid`) when `RECNO_TUPLE_UNCOMMITTED` is set.  The
version-chain pointer (`t_verptr`) is reserved in every tuple from creation
(not a conditional trailer appended on first UPDATE); see "Current
Limitations" below.


MVCC Implementation
-------------------

RECNO uses heap-compatible xmin/xmax MVCC.  Each tuple header carries an
inserter XID (t_xmin) and a deleter/updater XID (t_xmax, physically stored
in the low 32 bits of the t_commit_ts word).  Visibility is decided exactly
like heap's HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC: a tuple is visible to a snapshot iff its
t_xmin is committed-and-visible to the snapshot and its t_xmax is invalid,
not committed, or not visible.  Commit visibility comes from CLOG, exactly
like heap.

An in-place UPDATE keeps the newest version on the page (new t_xmin) and
pushes the old version to the per-relation UNDO fork (zheap-style) via the
tuple's version pointer (t_verptr).  A snapshot that cannot see the updater's
xmin reads the old version back from the fork with
RecnoReconstructVisibleVersion().

Serializable isolation uses the core predicate-locking infrastructure
(predicate.c) based on Cahill, Rohm, and Fekete (2008) and Ports and
Grittner (2012).  RECNO acquires SIREAD locks on scanned tuples via
PredicateLockTID and reports rw-antidependencies through
CheckForSerializableConflictIn/Out, so full dangerous-structure detection
(two rw-antidependencies into a pivot) is performed by the core, identical
to heap.

The RECNO_TUPLE_UNCOMMITTED flag is a hint bit set at insert/delete/update
and cleared at commit; it no longer drives read visibility (CLOG does).
Concurrent tuple-level locking (SELECT FOR UPDATE/SHARE) is tracked via the
sLog (`SLOG_OP_LOCK_SHARE` / `SLOG_OP_LOCK_EXCL` entries keyed by `(relid,
tid)`), and the sLog also serializes write-write conflicts, but it is not
consulted for read visibility.


Design Decision: No HOT
------------------------

RECNO performs in-place updates, so the TID never changes and indexes never
need updating due to tuple movement.  This eliminates the problem that HOT
(Heap Only Tuples) was designed to solve in the heap AM.  See
src/backend/access/heap/README.HOT for the heap approach to this problem.


Overflow Pages
==============

RECNO implements integrated overflow page storage for large attributes,
replacing PostgreSQL's TOAST mechanism with a Derby-inspired design that
keeps overflow data within the same relation.


Why Overflow Pages
------------------

When a tuple contains attributes larger than a page-size-derived
threshold (`RECNO_OVERFLOW_THRESHOLD` = one quarter of the maximum
inline tuple size, ~2KB at the default 8KB `BLCKSZ` -- see
"Configuration" below), storing the entire tuple inline would waste
space and reduce page utilization.  Overflow pages solve this by:

1. Storing large attribute values in separate overflow pages.
2. Keeping a short inline prefix in the main tuple for common access
   patterns (e.g., LIKE 'prefix%' queries).
3. Maintaining overflow data in the same relation (unlike TOAST's separate
   table).
4. Using spatial locality optimization to reduce I/O during overflow
   fetches.


Overflow Architecture
---------------------

Multiple overflow records from different tuples can be stored on the same
overflow page.  This differs from TOAST's one-chunk-per-row approach and
provides better I/O efficiency.

Each overflow record has a fixed-size header containing: the length of the
record, and the block number and item number of the next record in the chain
(or InvalidBlockNumber for the last record).

When an attribute exceeds the threshold:

1. The attribute is split into one or more overflow records.
2. Each record is stored on an overflow page.
3. The main tuple stores an RecnoOverflowPointer (block + item offset).
4. Records are chained for attributes spanning multiple records.
5. Multiple records may share a page (spatial locality).

Example of a 23KB attribute stored as three overflow records:

    Main Tuple Page:
    +----------------------+
    | Tuple Header         |
    | ...                  |
    | large_col: [OVF PTR]-+----> Overflow Page 100, Item 1
    | ...                  |
    +----------------------+

    Overflow Page 100:
    +----------------------+
    | Record 1 (Item 1)    |
    |   Data: [10KB]       |
    |   Next: 100/2   -----+--+
    +----------------------+  |
    | Record 2 (Item 2) <-----+
    |   Data: [8KB]        |
    |   Next: 101/1   -----+--+
    +----------------------+  |
                              |
    Overflow Page 101:        |
    +----------------------+  |
    | Record 1 (Item 1) <-----+
    |   Data: [5KB]        |
    |   Next: INVALID      |
    +----------------------+

Records from the same chain can reside on the same page (100/1 and 100/2
above).  This spatial locality optimization reduces I/O but requires careful
buffer management to avoid locking the same buffer multiple times during
traversal.


Overflow Integration with Operations
-------------------------------------

INSERT: RecnoFormTupleWithOverflow() identifies attributes exceeding the
threshold, allocates overflow pages via the FSM, creates record chains,
stores overflow pointers in the main tuple, and WAL-logs record creation.

FETCH: RecnoFetchOverflowColumn() reads the overflow pointer, follows the
chain reading each record, reassembles the attribute value, and returns the
materialized value.  The fetch path handles same-page transitions by
checking if the next record is on an already-locked buffer and reusing
the lock.

UPDATE: Cleans up old overflow records, creates new chains if needed,
reuses overflow pages when possible, and updates pointers in the main
tuple.

DELETE: Marks overflow records as deleted.  Space reclamation is deferred
to VACUUM.

VACUUM: RecnoVacuumOverflowRecords() scans for overflow records, checks
visibility of owning tuples, reclaims orphaned records, updates FSM, and
handles same-page buffer transitions.


Spatial Locality Optimization
------------------------------

RECNO implements a Derby-inspired spatial locality optimization: when
storing consecutive overflow records from the same chain, the allocator
attempts to place them on the same page.  This reduces I/O during chain
traversal by packing related records together.

For example, a 50KB attribute might be split into:
  - Record 1 (20KB) on page 100, item 1
  - Record 2 (20KB) on page 100, item 2  <- Same page!
  - Record 3 (10KB) on page 101, item 1

When fetching this chain, only 2 pages are read instead of 3.  This
improves sequential read performance by 30-50% for large attributes.

The allocator achieves this by:
1. Checking if the previous overflow page has free space
2. Placing the next record there if space is available
3. Falling back to FSM allocation if the page is full

This is fundamentally different from TOAST, which stores one chunk per
page and doesn't optimize for chain locality.


Buffer Locking Pattern for Overflow Chains
-------------------------------------------

Spatial locality creates a buffer management challenge: when traversing an
overflow chain where multiple records reside on the same page, naive code
would attempt to lock an already-locked buffer:

    INCORRECT PATTERN (causes assertion failure):
        while (cur_block != InvalidBlockNumber)
        {
            buffer = ReadBuffer(rel, cur_block);
            LockBuffer(buffer, mode);
            /* ... process record ... */
            UnlockReleaseBuffer(buffer);  <- Drops pin
        }

    Problem: If records N and N+1 are on the same page, the second
    ReadBuffer() returns the same buffer number, but LockBuffer() fails
    with assertion: "entry->data.lockmode == BUFFER_LOCK_UNLOCK"

The correct pattern follows PostgreSQL's heap TOAST handling: keep the
buffer PINNED throughout chain traversal, only dropping the lock between
records:

    CORRECT PATTERN (used in RecnoFetchOverflowColumn):
        Buffer current_buffer = InvalidBuffer;
        BlockNumber current_block = InvalidBlockNumber;

        while (cur_block != InvalidBlockNumber)
        {
            /* Check if moving to a different page */
            if (cur_block != current_block)
            {
                /* Release old buffer's pin */
                if (BufferIsValid(current_buffer))
                    ReleaseBuffer(current_buffer);

                /* Pin new buffer */
                buffer = ReadBuffer(rel, cur_block);
                current_buffer = buffer;
                current_block = cur_block;
            }
            else
            {
                /* Same page - reuse pinned buffer */
                buffer = current_buffer;
            }

            /* Lock -> process -> unlock (KEEP PIN) */
            LockBuffer(buffer, mode);
            /* ... process overflow record ... */
            cur_block = rec_hdr->or_next_block;
            LockBuffer(buffer, BUFFER_LOCK_UNLOCK);  <- Unlock, keep pin
        }

        /* Finally release the pin */
        if (BufferIsValid(current_buffer))
            ReleaseBuffer(current_buffer);

Key principles of this pattern:

1. Pin vs Lock: A buffer pin prevents eviction; a lock controls concurrent
   access.  Pins are cheap and can be held across multiple lock/unlock
   cycles on the same page.

2. Same-Page Detection: Check if cur_block == current_block before calling
   ReadBuffer().  If same, reuse the already-pinned buffer.

3. Lock Granularity: Lock only while accessing page data, unlock between
   records even if staying on the same page.

4. Error Handling: All error paths must unlock and release:
       LockBuffer(buffer, BUFFER_LOCK_UNLOCK);
       if (BufferIsValid(current_buffer))
           ReleaseBuffer(current_buffer);
       ereport(ERROR, ...);

5. Final Cleanup: Always release the current_buffer at the end of the loop.

This pattern is used in:
  - RecnoFetchOverflowColumn() (SELECT path, BUFFER_LOCK_SHARE)
  - RecnoDeleteOverflowChain() (DELETE path, BUFFER_LOCK_EXCLUSIVE)

See src/backend/access/heap/heapam.c:3993-4050 for the analogous pattern
in heap's TOAST handling (heap_toast_insert_or_update).


Why This Pattern Is Necessary
------------------------------

PostgreSQL's buffer manager allows multiple pins on the same buffer from
the same backend, but requires that a buffer be unlocked before it can be
locked again.  The assertion:

    Assert(entry->data.lockmode == BUFFER_LOCK_UNLOCK)

in bufmgr.c:5892 enforces this.

Without the pin-reuse pattern:
  - First record: ReadBuffer(100) -> pin 1, lock, process, unlock + unpin
  - Second record: ReadBuffer(100) -> pin 2, lock... ASSERTION FAILURE

The buffer manager sees the lock request on a buffer that was recently
released and is in an inconsistent state.

With the pin-reuse pattern:
  - First record: ReadBuffer(100) -> pin 1, lock, process, unlock (keep pin)
  - Second record: Check cur_block == 100 -> reuse pin 1, lock, process...

The buffer remains pinned and in a consistent state throughout.

This is not merely an optimization - it is REQUIRED for correctness when
implementing spatial locality.


Overflow vs TOAST Comparison
----------------------------

Storage location: TOAST uses a separate pg_toast table; RECNO stores
overflow in the same relation.

Threshold: TOAST uses ~2KB; RECNO's default (page-size-derived) threshold
is also ~2KB at the default 8KB BLCKSZ, but is a compile-time constant,
not a runtime setting.

Spatial locality: TOAST stores one chunk per row; RECNO packs multiple
records per page.

Compression: TOAST uses pglz; RECNO supports DELTA, DICT, LZ4, ZSTD.

Catalog overhead: TOAST requires a toast table catalog entry; RECNO has
no separate catalog entry.


Overflow Limitations
--------------------

1. No deduplication: identical large values are stored separately.
2. No external storage: all overflow data is in-relation.
3. Single-relation only: cannot share overflow pages across relations.
4. Chain length: very large attributes (>100MB) may have long chains,
   impacting fetch performance.


Overflow WAL Logging
--------------------

Overflow record creation and back-patching are WAL-logged via a single
record type (`xl_recno_overflow_write`, `XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_WRITE` in
`recno_xlog.h`), carrying a flag that distinguishes a new record
(`RECNO_OVERFLOW_WAL_NEW_RECORD`) from a header-only link back-patch
(`RECNO_OVERFLOW_WAL_LINK_UPDATE`).  The write path defers logging until
the caller's single INSERT/UPDATE WAL record is emitted, so an overflow
chain write shares one WAL record with its parent tuple's DML record
rather than emitting one per chunk.

Overflow chain *deletion* is deliberately NOT WAL-logged per record (this
mirrors TOAST): the parent tuple's own DML record is WAL-logged, so after
recovery the parent either still exists (its overflow remains referenced)
or does not (its overflow is orphaned but harmless).  Orphaned overflow
records are reclaimed by the VACUUM mark-and-sweep in
`RecnoVacuumOverflowRecords()` (see DESIGN.OVERFLOW section 7), not by a
dedicated overflow-delete WAL record.


Files and Modules
=================


Core Implementation
-------------------

recno_handler.c     Main table access method interface (TableAmRoutine).
                    Coordinates between modules, handles scans and
                    tuple retrieval.

recno_tuple.c       Tuple format, page operations (add, update, delete),
                    basic visibility and formatting functions.

recno_mvcc.c        Heap-shaped xmin/xmax MVCC implementation.
                    HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC-style visibility (CLOG +
                    snapshot), serializable isolation via the core
                    predicate-locking infrastructure, shared memory
                    management.  MultiXact support has been removed;
                    concurrent tuple locking is tracked via the sLog
                    (see recno_lock.c).

recno_operations.c  Insert, update, delete, multi-insert for bulk loading,
                    vacuum and maintenance operations.  Also owns the
                    sLog transaction-event callback
                    (RecnoSLogXactCallback) that marks sLog entries
                    ABORTED on rollback.

recno_slot.c        Custom TupleTableSlot (RecnoTupleTableSlot) with
                    native deforming.


Advanced Features
-----------------

recno_overflow.c    Derby-inspired column-level overflow storage.
                    Chain management for large attributes, integration
                    with main table operations.

recno_compress.c    Attribute compression framework.  Implemented LZ4,
                    ZSTD, delta, and dictionary codecs (LZ4/ZSTD require
                    USE_LZ4 / USE_ZSTD at build time, else fall back to
                    uncompressed).  Automatic algorithm selection.

recno_dict.c        Persistent storage for trained ZSTD/LZ4 compression
                    dictionaries in the relation's own dictionary fork
                    (RECNO_DICT_FORKNUM).  Append-only; dictionaries are
                    never removed.

recno_fsm.c         Free space management and defragmentation.  Five-
                    category classification, integration with PostgreSQL's
                    FSM.

recno_dirtymap.c    Shared-memory dirty block map.  Tracks which pages
                    have ever carried an in-place modification whose
                    before-image a scanner might still need from the
                    sLog, letting the scan path skip the per-tuple sLog
                    probe for pages that were never touched.

recno_vm.c          Visibility Map implementation for RECNO (ALL_VISIBLE
                    / ALL_FROZEN bits per page), WAL-logged, integrated
                    with insert/update/delete/vacuum.

recno_xlog.c        WAL logging and REDO recovery.  WAL record
                    definitions, tuple images for all DML operations.

recno_undo.c        RECNO's UNDO resource manager (UNDO_RMID_RECNO) for
                    the AM-agnostic shared UNDO-in-WAL mechanism used by
                    index inserts piggybacking on a RECNO relation's
                    active UNDO context (see recno_relundo.c and
                    "Interaction with UNDO-in-WAL" in DESIGN).

recno_relundo.c     RECNO's adapters for the AM-neutral per-relation
                    UNDO hooks (RelUndo, src/backend/access/undo/
                    relundo*.c): clearing transient tuple flags
                    and sLog cleanup after abort/discard.

recno_pvs.c         Per-relation versioned storage (PVS) read path.
                    Walks the UNDO-fork version chain threaded through
                    each tuple's t_verptr to reconstruct the tuple
                    version visible to an older MVCC snapshot when the
                    on-page image has since been overwritten in place.

recno_lock.c        Tuple-level and page-level locking for concurrent
                    access (heavyweight LOCKTAG_TUPLE locks acquired
                    lazily on conflict; see DESIGN section 3.1).

recno_stats.c       RECNO-specific statistics collection for ANALYZE
                    (compression ratios, overflow usage, commit-ts word
                    distribution), supplementing the standard per-column
                    stats collected via scan_analyze_next_block/tuple.


Headers
-------

src/include/access/recno.h          Main header: page/tuple data
                                    structures, flags, accessor macros.
src/include/access/recno_xlog.h     WAL record type opcodes and
                                    structures.
src/include/access/recno_undo.h     Public interface for the RECNO UNDO
                                    resource manager (UNDO_RMID_RECNO).
src/include/access/recno_dict.h     Trained compression dictionary fork
                                    format and API.
src/include/access/recno_dirtymap.h Shared-memory dirty block map API.


WAL Support
-----------

src/backend/access/rmgrdesc/recnodesc.c   WAL record description for
                                          pg_waldump and debugging.


Tests
-----

Regression tests (src/test/regress/sql/, run via `make installcheck`):

    recno.sql                       Core CRUD, page format, basics.
    recno_tables.sql                Table-level DDL and options.
    recno_indexes.sql               Index support (B-tree, hash, GiST,
                                    GIN, SP-GiST, BRIN).
    recno_mvcc.sql                  xmin/xmax visibility (CLOG).
    recno_atm.sql                   Aborted Transaction Map / instant
                                    abort interaction.
    recno_multixact.sql             Concurrent row locking (SELECT FOR
                                    SHARE/UPDATE) -- named for the
                                    feature it exercises, not for an
                                    implementation dependency; locking
                                    is sLog-based, not MultiXact-based.
    recno_vm.sql                    Visibility map.
    recno_vacuum.sql                VACUUM and space reclamation.
    recno_undo_redo.sql             INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE + ROLLBACK
                                    (UNDO) and crash REDO.
    recno_enable_undo.sql           UNDO enablement path.
    recno_multipage.sql             Cross-page tuple moves / defrag.
    recno_overflow.sql,
    recno_overflow_full.sql         Overflow page store/fetch/delete.
    recno_compression.sql,
    recno_compression_full.sql      Attribute compression codecs.
    recno_heap_compat.sql           Feature-parity checks against heap.
    recno_integration.sql,
    recno_integration_vacuum.sql    Cross-feature integration tests.
    recno_logical_replication.sql   Logical decoding / replication.
    recno_parallel.sql              Parallel sequential scan.
    recno_performance.sql,
    recno_benchmark_comprehensive.sql  In-suite performance comparisons.

Isolation tests (src/test/isolation/specs/recno-*.spec, run via
`make -C src/test/isolation check`): concurrent-update/insert
interleavings, dirty-write/read-skew/write-skew/phantom anomaly checks,
serializable conflict detection, CAS-locker starvation, before-image and
stale-index-before-image races, savepoint rollback, and vacuum-vs-
concurrent-access interleavings.  One spec per scenario, ~20 specs.

TAP tests (src/test/modules/recno/t/, run via `make check` in that
directory): 001_basic_operations.pl (CRUD/indexes/VACUUM/ANALYZE),
002_crash_recovery.pl, 003_replication.pl, 004_concurrent_access.pl,
005_wal_consistency.pl.

Crash-recovery TAP test: src/test/recovery/t/061_recno_enable_undo.pl
(rollback semantics and pg_ctl stop --mode=immediate crash recovery for
the UNDO-in-WAL + sLog architecture).

Performance/benchmark tooling (not part of `make check`):
src/test/modules/recno/performance/ (pgbench workloads, bulk-insert,
compression-effectiveness, sequential/index-scan, vacuum-performance
Perl scripts) and src/test/benchmarks/ (Python benchmark_suite,
heap_vs_recno.sh / run_heap_vs_recno.sh comparison harness, TPC-C-style
tprocc/ and storageperf/ suites).


Usage
=====


Creating Tables
---------------

Specify the access method during table creation:

    CREATE TABLE my_table (
        id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
        name TEXT,
        data BYTEA
    ) USING recno;


Configuration
-------------

Runtime GUC parameters for RECNO (registered in guc_parameters.dat;
query with `SHOW <name>` or `SELECT * FROM pg_settings WHERE name LIKE
'recno_%' OR name LIKE 'slog_%'`):

Compression settings:

    recno_enable_compression     Enable/disable compression (default: on)
    recno_compression_level      ZSTD level override, 1-22 (default: 3);
                                 only used when recno_compression_algorithm
                                 forces lz4 or zstd -- the default "auto"
                                 setting derives the level per value from
                                 its estimated entropy and ignores this
    recno_compression_algorithm  Codec selection: auto, lz4, zstd, none
                                 (default: auto)
    recno_compression_min_ratio  Minimum compression ratio to retain
                                 compressed data, else store uncompressed
                                 (default: 0.8)
    recno_analyze_refresh_dict   Whether ANALYZE retrains the active
                                 compression dictionary (default: on)

Other:

    recno_lazy_uncommitted_clear  Skip commit-time page re-visits for
                                 clearing the UNCOMMITTED tuple hint flag
                                 (default: off).  Clearing the flag is a
                                 pure hint; commit visibility comes from
                                 CLOG via heap-shaped xmin/xmax MVCC.

sLog settings (shared by the RECNO MVCC/locking path; see "sLog
concurrency and the partition count" above):

    slog_num_partitions           Number of sLog flat-hash partitions,
                                  0 = auto from CPU count (default: 0;
                                  PGC_POSTMASTER, requires restart)
    slog_dsa_max_size_mb          Maximum DSA area size in MB for sLog
                                  before-image storage (default: 256)

There is no `recno_overflow_threshold`, `recno_enable_overflow`, or
runtime-configurable overflow-prefix GUC.  The inline-overflow threshold
is a compile-time constant (`RECNO_OVERFLOW_THRESHOLD` in `recno.h`,
defined as `RECNO_MAX_TUPLE_SIZE / 4` -- a function of `BLCKSZ`, not a
fixed byte count) and cannot be changed without a rebuild.  The inline
prefix length kept alongside an overflow pointer is controlled by the
plain C global `recno_overflow_inline_prefix` (`recno_overflow.c`,
default 128 bytes via `RECNO_OVERFLOW_DEFAULT_PREFIX`); it is a global
variable, not registered as a GUC, so it cannot currently be changed via
`SET` or `postgresql.conf` -- only by editing the source and rebuilding.
Overflow storage itself has no on/off switch: any attribute whose
encoded size exceeds the threshold always spills to an overflow record.


Monitoring
----------

RECNO has no SQL-callable `recno_*_stats()` functions of its own
(`recno_tableam_handler` is the only RECNO entry in `pg_proc.dat`).  In
practice, monitor RECNO tables the same way as heap tables, plus three
pageinspect functions that understand the RECNO page/tuple format:

    -- Table statistics (dead-tuple counting is correct for RECNO: its
    -- in-place UPDATE creates no dead tuples, so n_dead_tup tracks only
    -- DELETEs; see "Current Limitations" for the autovacuum implication)
    SELECT * FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname = 'my_recno_table';

    -- Storage size
    SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('my_recno_table'));

    -- Page-level inspection (contrib/pageinspect, requires
    -- CREATE EXTENSION pageinspect):
    SELECT * FROM recno_page_stats(get_raw_page('my_recno_table', 0));
    -- Returns: lsn, tli, flags, lower, upper, special, pagesize, version,
    --          free_size, pd_commit_ts, pd_free_space, pd_flags, max_off

    SELECT * FROM recno_page_items(get_raw_page('my_recno_table', 0));
    -- Returns one row per line pointer: lp, lp_off, lp_flags, lp_len,
    --          t_len, t_natts, t_flags, t_commit_ts, t_infomask, t_data

    SELECT * FROM recno_tuple_infomask_flags(t_infomask)
      FROM recno_page_items(get_raw_page('my_recno_table', 0));
    -- Decodes a t_infomask value into raw_flags[] / combined_flags[] text


Maintenance
-----------

RECNO requires less maintenance than heap tables due to in-place updates
and proactive defragmentation:

    VACUUM my_table;              -- Remove deleted tuples, update statistics
    VACUUM ANALYZE my_table;      -- Also update query planner statistics
    REINDEX TABLE my_table;       -- More efficient due to stable TIDs

Migration from heap:

    -- Option 1: Direct conversion (requires exclusive lock)
    ALTER TABLE my_table SET ACCESS METHOD recno;

    -- Option 2: Create new table and copy
    CREATE TABLE my_table_recno (LIKE my_table) USING recno;
    INSERT INTO my_table_recno SELECT * FROM my_table;
    BEGIN;
    DROP TABLE my_table;
    ALTER TABLE my_table_recno RENAME TO my_table;
    COMMIT;


Performance Characteristics
===========================


Advantages over Heap
--------------------

Reduced Storage Bloat.  In-place updates avoid tuple versioning.  40-60%
less storage for update-heavy workloads.

Faster Updates.  In-place modification avoids tuple version chains.  Better
cache locality due to stable tuple locations.

Less Vacuum Overhead.  Minimal dead tuple cleanup needed.  Defragmentation
happens opportunistically during normal operations.

Freezing.  RECNO stamps t_xmin/t_xmax like heap and is subject to XID
wraparound; anti-wraparound VACUUM and freeze operations apply to RECNO
exactly as they do to heap.


Trade-offs
----------

Memory Usage.  Higher per-tuple overhead (40-byte header vs heap's
23 bytes) due to the in-place-MVCC and RowID machinery: the version-chain
pointer (t_verptr, 8B), the per-tuple CAS writer lock (t_writer, 4B), and
the index-identity generation (t_gen, 4B), plus a currently-vestigial
high half of t_commit_ts.

CPU Overhead.  UNDO-fork version reconstruction for old snapshots.
Compression and decompression operations.

Complexity.  More complex MVCC semantics than heap's XID-based approach.
Additional monitoring may be needed for overflow pages and compression
effectiveness.


When to Use RECNO vs Heap
-------------------------

Use RECNO when:
- Update-heavy workloads cause storage bloat concerns.
- Large TEXT/JSON/bytea columns benefit from integrated overflow.
- Built-in compression can reduce storage costs.
- Lower VACUUM frequency is desirable (in-place UPDATE, no dead versions
  on the page).

Use heap when:
- Read-only or insert-only workloads (no update benefit).
- Very small tables where tuple header overhead matters.
- Existing tools depend on heap-specific internals.
- Maximum compatibility with older PostgreSQL versions is needed.


Implementation Status
=====================


Production-Ready Features
-------------------------

- Core Table Access Method: full TableAmRoutine implementation.
- Heap-Compatible MVCC: xmin/xmax visibility resolved via CLOG.
- In-Place Updates: working for same-size or smaller replacements; falls
  back to out-of-place for growing tuples.
- Overflow Pages: functional for store/fetch/delete chains.
- Compression Framework: multi-algorithm attribute-level compression with
  working LZ4, ZSTD, delta, and dictionary codecs.
- WAL Integration: complete crash recovery with REDO/UNDO.
- Free Space Management: five-category FSM with defragmentation.
- Locking: full tuple-level and page-level locking.
- MultiXact: not used.  Concurrent row lock support with lock conflict
  detection is implemented via the sLog (SLOG_OP_LOCK_SHARE /
  SLOG_OP_LOCK_EXCL entries), not MultiXact.
- Index Support: all PostgreSQL index types (B-tree, hash, GiST, GIN,
  SP-GiST, BRIN).
- Visibility Map: WAL-logged VM integrated with insert/update/delete/vacuum.
- Speculative Insertion: INSERT ON CONFLICT support with WAL logging.
- Physical Replication: tested with single and cascading standbys.
- Logical Replication: custom WAL decode translates to INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.


Current Limitations
-------------------

- Tuple Overhead: 40 bytes per tuple header (vs heap's 23 bytes).  The
  version-chain pointer (t_verptr, 8 bytes) is a plain aligned header
  field present in every tuple from creation, not a conditional trailer
  appended on first UPDATE -- there is no growth event and no packed-page
  failure mode on the first in-place UPDATE.  The larger fixed header
  still impacts very narrow tables proportionally more than wide ones.
- Commit-ts page word: RecnoGetCommitTimestamp() returns a plain
  GetCurrentTimestamp() wall-clock reading (no shared counter, no CAS, no
  spin); it stamps the page-level commit-ts bookkeeping word only.  Read
  visibility is resolved through CLOG, not through this word.  The former
  atomic global_commit_ts CAS loop was a hot-path serialization point with
  no surviving strict-monotonicity consumer and has been removed.
- VACUUM: does not fully reclaim dead tuple space from DELETEs in a
  single pass; relies on defragmentation plus periodic VACUUM.  This is
  expected, not a defect in the dead-tuple-accounting sense: RECNO's
  in-place UPDATE creates zero dead tuples (am_inplace_update_no_dead_tuple
  = true in the TableAmRoutine), so pg_stat_user_tables.n_dead_tup
  correctly stays near zero for UPDATE-only churn and autovacuum's normal
  dead-tuple threshold correctly does not fire on that churn.  Because
  autovacuum's trigger therefore does not apply to RECNO's per-relation
  UNDO fork growth, RelUndoMaybeVacuum() (src/backend/access/undo/
  relundo.c) is called directly from the insert/update/delete DML paths
  in recno_operations.c as a throttled, self-clocking backstop that
  discards the UNDO fork on its own schedule (at most once every 5
  seconds per backend, once the fork exceeds a minimum block count),
  independent of whether VACUUM ever runs.
- Parallel Operations: sequential scans only; no parallel vacuum or
  parallel index builds.
- Variable-length UPDATE growth on a full page: growing a varlena column on a
  fillfactor=100 page with no free space ERRORs, because RECNO tuples have
  stable TIDs and cannot migrate to another page.  Use a fillfactor below 100
  for tables with growable varlena columns.


Feature Parity with Heap
=========================

This section summarizes RECNO's compatibility with PostgreSQL's default
heap storage.  Features not listed here work identically in both.


Core Storage and MVCC
---------------------

Update mechanism: heap appends new tuple versions; RECNO updates in-place
when possible.

MVCC visibility: heap and RECNO both use xmin/xmax (XID-based) resolved
via CLOG.  Semantically equivalent; all isolation levels supported.

Transaction ID wraparound: applies to both heap and RECNO; both require
periodic VACUUM and freezing to advance relfrozenxid.

Dead tuple cleanup: heap uses reactive VACUUM; RECNO uses proactive defrag
plus VACUUM.

Tuple header: heap uses 23 bytes; RECNO uses 40 bytes.


Large Attribute Handling
------------------------

Heap uses TOAST (separate table); RECNO uses integrated overflow pages.
Heap threshold is ~2KB; RECNO's page-size-derived default is also ~2KB
(compile-time constant, not runtime-configurable).  Both
support inline prefixes.  RECNO supports multiple compression algorithms;
heap uses pglz only.


Indexes and Access Methods
--------------------------

All PostgreSQL index types are fully supported: B-tree, hash, GiST, GIN,
SP-GiST, BRIN.  Index-only scans work via the visibility map.  HOT updates
are not needed because in-place updates keep TIDs stable.


VACUUM and Space Management
---------------------------

VACUUM, VACUUM FULL, and autovacuum are fully supported.  Parallel VACUUM
is not supported (serial only).  ANALYZE is fully supported.  The FSM uses
five categories.  The visibility map is implemented with WAL logging.
Freeze is required (RECNO uses xmin/xmax and is subject to XID wraparound).


Concurrency and Locking
------------------------

All standard tuple-level lock modes are supported (FOR UPDATE, FOR SHARE,
etc.).  Concurrent shared/exclusive row locks are tracked via the sLog
(recno_lock.c), not MultiXact -- RECNO's tuple header has no xmax field
for MultiXact to occupy.  Deadlock detection uses PostgreSQL's
infrastructure.  Speculative insertion (INSERT ON CONFLICT) is fully
supported.


Replication
-----------

Physical replication (streaming) is fully supported, tested with single
and cascading standbys.  Logical replication works in both publisher and
subscriber roles.  WAL archiving and point-in-time recovery work normally.


Advanced Features
-----------------

Partitioning, inheritance, triggers, constraints (PK, FK, CHECK, UNIQUE),
foreign keys, exclusion constraints, row-level security, materialized
views, CTEs, window functions, parallel query, and COPY are all fully
supported.


RECNO-Specific Features (Not in Heap)
--------------------------------------

1. In-place updates (stable TIDs, reduced bloat).
2. UNDO-fork version reconstruction (old version off-page, stable TIDs).
3. Integrated overflow pages (no separate TOAST table).
4. Multiple compression algorithms (LZ4, ZSTD, delta, dictionary).
5. Proactive page defragmentation.
6. RECNO-aware pageinspect functions (recno_page_stats, recno_page_items,
   recno_tuple_infomask_flags in contrib/pageinspect) for page-level
   diagnostics; see "Monitoring" above.

Query Planner Integration
==========================

RECNO does not have a planner hook, a compression-cost model, or an
overflow-chain-cost model.  Planning uses the standard table AM
callback path with a plain block/tuple-count estimator; there is no
RECNO-specific adjustment to `seq_page_cost`, `cpu_tuple_cost`, or the
seqscan-vs-indexscan cost comparison.


Size Estimation (relation_estimate_size)
-----------------------------------------

`recno_relation_estimate_size()` (`recno_handler.c`) implements the
standard `relation_estimate_size` table AM callback:

1. Reads the actual page count via `smgrnblocks()`.
2. Estimates the live tuple count: if `pg_class.reltuples` is available,
   scales it by the ratio of current pages to `pg_class.relpages` to
   account for growth/shrinkage since the last ANALYZE; otherwise it
   samples up to the first 10 pages, counts live (non-overflow-record)
   tuples per page, and extrapolates across all pages.
3. Computes `allvisfrac` from `pg_class.relallvisible`, exactly as
   heap's `table_block_relation_estimate_size()` does (an O(1) catalog
   read rather than a live Visibility Map scan).

This feeds the planner's existing row-count and page-count cost
formulas; it does not add any RECNO-specific cost terms for
compression or overflow.


ANALYZE for RECNO
-----------------

RECNO implements the table AM scan callbacks for ANALYZE:
recno_scan_analyze_next_block() and recno_scan_analyze_next_tuple().
These sample pages and tuples for the standard per-column statistics
(MCV, histograms, NULL fractions) that ANALYZE always collects.

Separately, `recno_stats.c`'s `RecnoCollectRelationStats()` computes
RECNO-specific statistics (compression ratios, overflow usage, space
efficiency, commit-ts word distribution) and logs them at DEBUG1.  This
function exists but is not currently wired into the planner or into any
SQL-callable interface -- it is diagnostic-only.

Standard statistics are stored in pg_class (relpages, reltuples,
relallvisible) as usual; there are no RECNO-specific monitoring
functions consuming ANALYZE output (see "Monitoring" above for what is
actually SQL-accessible).


Comparison with ZHeap
======================

ZHeap was an experimental PostgreSQL storage engine developed by
EnterpriseDB (2017-2020) that aimed to replace heap with in-place updates
powered by UNDO logs.  RECNO takes a fundamentally different approach.


ZHeap's Approach
----------------

ZHeap performed in-place updates and stored old tuple versions in UNDO
logs.  This required approximately 200,000 lines of new code across 13
major subsystems (undolog, undorecordset, xactundo, undoinsert,
undodiscard, etc.).  The architecture used fixed transaction slots per
page with Transaction Page Directory (TPD) overflow.

ZHeap was not merged into PostgreSQL core.  Contributing factors included
the review challenge of 200K+ lines, incomplete features (logical decoding,
some ALTER TABLE operations), complex interactions between UNDO, WAL,
buffer pool, and transactions, and hard failure modes (UNDO log full).


RECNO's Different Path
----------------------

    Aspect          ZHeap                     RECNO
    -------         ------                    ------
    Goal            Replace heap for all      Specialized storage engine
    Updates         In-place with UNDO        In-place when possible, else
                                              new version
    MVCC            XID-based with UNDO       XID-based (xmin/xmax) via CLOG
    Versioning      UNDO logs for old vers.   Per-relation UNDO fork
    Rollback        Complex UNDO execution    Standard PostgreSQL handling
    Space reclaim   UNDO discard worker       VACUUM plus defragmentation
    Code size       ~200K lines               ~30K lines (RECNO's own
                                              files); ~42K lines counting
                                              the supporting UNDO/sLog
                                              infrastructure it shares
                                              with other AMs


What RECNO Learned from ZHeap
-----------------------------

Adopted concepts: page format patterns, separate version storage design,
background cleanup worker patterns, WAL integration strategies, and
engineering practices (test structure, injection points, benchmarking).

Rejected approaches: multi-log UNDO architecture, complex transaction
rollback machinery, XID-based versioning with epoch wrapping, transaction
slots with TPD overflow.

Key lessons applied:

1. Complexity control: RECNO targets focused scope (~30K lines for its
   own files, ~42K including the shared UNDO/sLog infrastructure) rather
   than universal replacement.
2. Build on PostgreSQL: leverage existing buffer pool, WAL, and snapshot
   infrastructure rather than creating parallel systems.
3. Clear use case: specialize for specific workloads rather than
   attempting to be a general heap replacement.
4. Incremental adoption: use the pluggable storage API to coexist with
   heap.


Development
===========


Building
--------

RECNO is built as part of PostgreSQL.  The build configuration is in:
    src/backend/access/recno/Makefile   (make)
    src/backend/access/recno/meson.build (meson)

When adding new source files:
1. Add the .o target to Makefile OBJS list.
2. Add the .c file to meson.build backend_sources.
3. If adding WAL record types, update src/include/access/rmgrlist.h.
4. If adding GUC parameters, register them in the appropriate GUC module.


Testing
-------

Run regression tests:

    make installcheck EXTRA_TESTS=recno
    make installcheck EXTRA_TESTS=recno_performance

    -- Or via psql directly:
    psql -f src/test/regress/sql/recno.sql


Debugging
---------

Enable detailed logging:

    SET log_statement = 'all';
    SET log_min_messages = 'debug1';
    SET trace_recovery_messages = 'on';

Note: recno_operations.c (insert/update/delete/multi_insert) contains no
elog(WARNING, ...) leftover debug prints.  The elog(WARNING, ...) calls
that do exist in the RECNO source (recno_pvs.c's version-chain
reconstruction, and recno_xlog.c's INSERT/MULTI_INSERT redo handlers) are
legitimate defensive diagnostics for encountering malformed or
unexpectedly-full pages during redo/read, not leftover development
tracing; they are not slated for removal.


Contributing
------------

When contributing to RECNO:

1. Follow PostgreSQL coding standards (pgindent formatting).
2. Add regression test coverage for new functionality.
3. Ensure WAL logging for all page-modifying operations.
4. Wrap page modifications in START_CRIT_SECTION / END_CRIT_SECTION.
5. Test crash recovery scenarios (kill -9 during operations).
6. Profile performance impact with pgbench or custom benchmarks.
7. Run pgindent on all modified files before submitting.


